Localized facial erythema and swelling secondary to mechanical squeezing of comedones
Minor Ailments and Upsets
Focal cutaneous edema around comedone, Localized manipulation tenderness, Petechial bruising

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MAU-011
Reason for Visit: Acute localized facial swelling and erythema requiring urgent physical evaluation to rule out early facial cellulitis.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted rapidly evolving central facial swelling following skin trauma. Urgent physical assessment was strictly required prior to the next in-hours period to evaluate for spreading cellulitis within the facial danger triangle, avoiding potential cavernous sinus thrombosis or the need for intravenous antibiotics.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My face is swollen and painful where I squeezed a pimple this morning.
History of Present Illness: Patient describes a focal area of cutaneous edema and redness surrounding a central pilosebaceous unit evolving over the past 12 hours. Reports mild tenderness directly localized to the site of physical manipulation. Notes petechial bruising near the squeezed lesion and a disrupted epidermal barrier with a tiny central crust. Complete absence of fluctuant cystic formation. Denies systemic fevers or chills. Swelling has stabilized over the last 2 hours.
Functional Status: Fully independent.
Social/Safety Concerns: Health anxiety regarding facial appearance and potential for permanent scarring.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 124/80 | HR: 88 | Temp: 37.0 C | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Alert, focused on the facial lesion, no signs of systemic toxicity.
Physical Exam: Facial examination reveals focal cutaneous edema strictly limited to the area around the comedone, localized manipulation tenderness, and petechial bruising consistent with the diagnostic triad. Disrupted epidermal barrier with a tiny central crust is visible. Absence of fluctuant cystic formation. Lack of spreading cellulitis margins. No regional submandibular lymphadenopathy.
Environmental Observations: Magnifying mirror and multiple extraction tools present in the bathroom.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Intermittent use of over-the-counter salicylic acid washes.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Localized facial erythema and swelling secondary to mechanical squeezing of comedones - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Facial Cellulitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of spreading diffuse erythema, lack of deep tissue induration, and normal temperature.

Cutaneous Abscess - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete absence of palpable fluctuance or purulent drainage.

Cavernous Sinus Thrombosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal extraocular movements, absence of proptosis, and lack of systemic toxicity or headache.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, localized facial edema and erythema closely mimicking early facial cellulitis. Given the lesion's proximity to the facial danger triangle, delaying assessment until the regular clinic hours posed an unacceptable risk of failing to identify a rapidly spreading bacterial infection capable of intracranial extension. Urgent face-to-face assessment confirmed the localized, mechanical nature of the trauma, safely avoiding unnecessary broad-spectrum antibiotic initiation while providing definitive wound care advice.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Cleansing of the localized area with mild soap and water. Application of a cold compress to reduce acute mechanical edema. Advice on strict hands-off healing.
Positive Behavioral Change: Apply a hydrocolloid acne patch immediately upon noticing a comedone to establish a physical barrier against manual extraction, reinforcing the habit by keeping patches visible next to the bathroom mirror.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Routine GP review if swelling expands or red streaks appear.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to Emergency Department or call 000 if the redness spreads to the eye, if vision changes occur, if a high fever develops, or if the face becomes acutely painful to light touch.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is a mechanical soft tissue injury; secondary infection remains a risk if the barrier remains disrupted.
Clinical Course: Swelling and petechiae are expected to resolve over 48 to 72 hours provided no further mechanical trauma occurs.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if the red area doubles in size, if you develop a severe headache, or if pus begins draining from the site.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The swelling is a direct result of the physical pressure applied by your fingers crushing the small blood vessels under the skin, not a severe infection.
Actionable Steps: Do not touch, squeeze, or apply makeup to the affected area. Use a cold compress for 10 minutes every hour to reduce swelling.

